# ── calls ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ 6.7.11 — a const context's call is a call path like any other: another file's `private` const
# fn is refused and a `#deprecated` one warns, through `_callee_site_checks`, at the call's NAME
# token (whose file is the caller's — a parameter default's call is judged in its own file). Every
# const context (a top-level or local const, an array size, an enum value, `#assert`, a case
# label) ran both silently while `var X = pk();` was refused. Said only in a const context of its
# own: a const fn's body (`_ce_infn`) is compiled by the parser, which checks its calls once, and
# the definition check runs in SKIP mode. A refusal is the evaluator's own (`_ce_err`, and
# `_panic` as `_ce_badn` sets it), so the statement's cascade is swallowed. 1 = refused.
# CHANGELOG [6.7.11]
# 6.7.14 — and in SKIP mode too: the right operand of a short-circuit `1 || pk()` / `0 && pk()` and
# an if-expression's untaken arm name the call as surely as a taken one (the run-time path refuses
# `var X = 1 || pk();`), so `private` covers them and `#deprecated` warns. A const fn's body stays
# unchecked (above). `_ce_xpend_one` re-walks an arm whose first walk was not refused (a refusal
# leaves the arm unclassed, never re-walked), so its calls are judged as before and the per-site
# memories (`_vis_seen`, `_dep_seen`) print nothing new. CHANGELOG [6.7.14]
